        <div id="buying-for-life" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Do You Buy a Generator That Lasts?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Look at the engine before the wattage. A cast-iron cylinder sleeve, an overhead-valve design, a low-oil shutdown and, on bigger machines, an oil filter are the features that separate a 1,000-hour engine from a 3,000-hour one. Manufacturers that sell their engines separately for commercial equipment tend to put the same engines in their generators, and their spec sheets say so.
          </p>

          <p>
            Read the warranty as a signal of what the maker expects. Residential portable warranties commonly run two to three years, and premium inverter brands often sit at the top of that range. Standby generator warranties are typically five years limited for residential use, with some brands offering extended plans; Kohler, for example, has quoted residential terms in years and hours. Check the current terms on the maker's site before you buy, because they change, and note any hour caps and registration requirements.
          </p>

          <p>
            Size it so it runs at half to three-quarters load during your normal outage. A generator that strains at its limit every time the furnace starts will wear faster than one with headroom, and a monster unit that idles near empty wastes fuel. Our <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">outage generator guide</a> walks through sizing from your actual loads. Fuel choice matters too: propane and natural gas do not go stale, which removes the biggest cause of early failure.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plan storage before you buy. A portable that lives in a dry shed or garage, away from the car and its exhaust, with a cover and a fuel plan, will outlast an identical one left under a tarp. For a standby unit, placement on a raised pad with the clearances the manual specifies, away from sprinklers and drifting snow, gives the enclosure and electronics their best chance at 20-plus years.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy from a brand with a parts network near you. Being able to order a carburetor or a controller board quickly is the difference between a two-day fix and a dead machine. That is the quiet advantage of the big names, and it is a better predictor of real-world lifespan than any single number on the box.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes With Generator Lifespan
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing Untreated Gas in the Carburetor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ethanol fuel starts breaking down within weeks and varnishes the carburetor's small jets. This is the single most common reason portables refuse to start after a summer in the garage. Add stabilizer and run it through, or run the carburetor dry before storage.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Break-In Oil Change</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A new engine sheds metal particles during its first hours. Many manuals call for the first oil change after roughly 5 to 25 hours for exactly this reason. Running a fresh generator through a long outage on factory oil starts its life with avoidable wear.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never Running It Between Outages</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A generator that sits for a year dries out seals, lets the battery die and hides problems until the night you need it. A monthly 15 to 30 minute run under some load keeps it ready and catches issues while repairs are still easy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running at Full Load for Days</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pulling the full rated watts for long periods overheats the engine and alternator. Size the machine so normal outage loads sit around half to three-quarters of its rating, and stagger big motor loads like well pumps and air conditioners.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Hour Meter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Oil intervals, valve checks and resale value all depend on run hours, not months. Owners who never read the meter tend to change oil too late after a long outage. Check it after every outage and log the number with the date.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many hours will a portable generator last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most mid-range portable generators are commonly rated around 1,000 to 2,000 run hours before needing major engine work, while budget open-frame units often sit closer to 500 to 1,000. Quality inverter models built on commercial-grade engines are frequently quoted at 3,000 to 5,000 hours. Maintenance and fuel care move a machine toward the top or bottom of its range.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many years does a whole-house generator last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A maintained home standby generator commonly lasts 20 to 30 years. Air-cooled units are usually rated for about 10,000 to 20,000 hours and liquid-cooled units for 20,000 to 30,000 or more. Because a typical home logs only 50 to 150 hours a year, calendar wear on electronics, seals and the battery often matters as much as engine hours.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is 1,000 hours a lot for a generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the type. On a budget portable, 1,000 hours is near the end of its expected engine life. On a quality inverter it is roughly a quarter of the way through, and on a home standby unit it is barely broken in. Ask what maintenance was done during those hours before judging a used machine by the number alone.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should a generator be serviced?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Portables typically need an oil change every 50 to 100 hours or once a season, plus air filter and spark plug checks; the first change comes after a short break-in period. Standby units usually get an annual professional service covering oil, filters, plugs, battery and the transfer switch. Always follow the intervals in your own owner's manual.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does running a generator without load hurt it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Short no-load runs, such as a standby generator's weekly exercise, are normal and do no harm. Long periods at very light load are less ideal on larger engines because they never reach full operating temperature, which can encourage carbon build-up. For monthly test runs on a portable, plug in a modest load like a work light or space heater.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a generator sit for years and still work?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can, if it was stored properly: carburetor run dry or fuel stabilized, fresh oil, battery on a maintainer and kept somewhere dry. Generators stored with untreated gasoline usually need carburetor cleaning before they will run. Expect to replace the battery and check fuel lines on any machine that has sat for several years.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do inverter generators last longer than regular ones?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Quality inverter generators often do, for two reasons. Their engines slow down at light loads instead of holding a fixed 3,600 rpm, so they accumulate less wear per hour, and premium brands use commercial-grade engines. Budget inverters do not always share those advantages, so check the engine specification and warranty rather than assuming.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

